WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.601
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy, Grammy and Tony

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:06.000
award-winning composer,
playwright, and the creator

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.767
of hit Broadway shows "Hamilton"
and "In the Heights."

00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:11.667
His feature directorial debut,
"tick, tick...BOOM!"

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:13.067
is now on Netflix.

00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:15.601
And you can hear his new music
in Disney's "Encanto,"

00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:17.567
which is in theaters everywhere
tomorrow.

00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:18.734
Let's take a look.

00:00:18.734 --> 00:00:21.467
-♪ Welcome to
the Family Madrigal ♪

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.100
♪ The home of
the Family Madrigal ♪

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.501
-♪ We're on our way ♪

00:00:25.501 --> 00:00:29.000
-♪ Where all the people are
fantastical and magical ♪

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.133
♪ I'm part of
the Family Madrigal ♪

00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:33.868
-Oh, my gosh, it's them!
What are the gifts?

00:00:33.868 --> 00:00:36.267
-I can't remember all the gifts!
-But I don't know who is who!

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:37.701
-Alright, alright, relax!

00:00:37.701 --> 00:00:39.801
-It is physically impossible
to relax!

00:00:39.801 --> 00:00:41.400
-Tell us everything!
What are your powers?

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.133
-Just tell us
what everyone can do!

00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:45.167
-And that's why
coffee's for grown-ups.

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:46.801
[ Laughter ]
-Please welcome

00:00:46.801 --> 00:00:48.901
back to the show,
our friend, Lin-Manuel Miranda.

00:00:48.901 --> 00:00:51.968
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:57.767
♪♪

00:00:57.767 --> 00:01:03.634
♪♪

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:05.334
Welcome back!
-Thank you.

00:01:05.334 --> 00:01:08.467
Thank you for having me.
-A lot a lot has happened.

00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:10.667
-A lot has happened.
[ Cheers and applause continue ]

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:13.267
Whoa, thank you. You're so kind.
Thank you.

00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:15.300
[ Laughs ]

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.300
Well.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.434
-So, one thing
I want to ask about, you know,

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:23.534
doing a Disney film like this,
a lot of pressure

00:01:23.534 --> 00:01:25.367
insofar as, "How are kids
going to like it?"

00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:27.834
You were saying backstage
that you did "Moana,"

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:30.701
that was the first movie
your oldest ever saw.

00:01:30.701 --> 00:01:33.434
-Yes, I found out
I got the job on "Moana" --

00:01:33.434 --> 00:01:36.434
I Interviewed for it,
and I found out I got the job

00:01:36.434 --> 00:01:38.968
the same day we found out
we were going to be parents.

00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:42.167
So it's all mixed up.
Like, "Moana" is, weirdly,

00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:44.300
my oldest son's sibling.
-I would imagine,

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.133
when he saw "Moana"
and liked it, that's great.

00:01:46.133 --> 00:01:47.868
But he also hadn't seen
anything else, right?

00:01:47.868 --> 00:01:49.334
-Yeah, he had nothing
to compare it to.

00:01:49.334 --> 00:01:50.734
-So he might've just been like,
"Look,

00:01:50.734 --> 00:01:52.000
I just like
the idea of movies."

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.300
-Yes.
Yes, this concept of -- of

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.734
images on a screen is --
-So, now he's a little older.

00:01:56.734 --> 00:01:58.534
I'm assuming, between
this and "Encanto,"

00:01:58.534 --> 00:02:00.801
he's seen some other movies.
-He has serious sophistication.

00:02:00.801 --> 00:02:02.734
-Yeah, he might have
some standards now.

00:02:02.734 --> 00:02:04.033
So you must -- Were you
more stressful

00:02:04.033 --> 00:02:05.767
when you showed him this one?
-100%.

00:02:05.767 --> 00:02:07.234
Well, you have to understand,
also,

00:02:07.234 --> 00:02:08.901
I wrote this
during the pandemic,

00:02:08.901 --> 00:02:12.467
so they had to hear
every phase of "Encanto"

00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:15.467
as I was writing the songs,
as Daddy is talking to himself

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:17.133
in the corner, singing...

00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:19.267
♪ Welcome to
the Family Madrigal ♪

00:02:19.267 --> 00:02:21.067
But I also have --
I mean, I have --

00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:22.367
I have a 3-year-old
and a 7-year-old.

00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:25.634
They are the greatest
beta testers in the world.

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:27.734
There were moments
in early drafts

00:02:27.734 --> 00:02:29.801
that were too scary for them.

00:02:29.801 --> 00:02:31.901
My 3-year-old has
the immortal quote.

00:02:31.901 --> 00:02:33.934
At one point, he grabs me
and he goes, "Daddy,

00:02:33.934 --> 00:02:36.868
can we watch a kids' movie?"
[ Laughter ]

00:02:36.868 --> 00:02:38.834
And I went back to the director.
I was like,

00:02:38.834 --> 00:02:40.601
"Okay, that seems too scary.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:40.601 --> 00:02:41.934
We got to go back
to the drawing board."

00:02:41.934 --> 00:02:44.634
But, yeah, it was great.
-Stephanie Beatriz.

00:02:44.634 --> 00:02:47.567
Stephanie Beatriz is
a voice in this.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.501
-Mirabel is our Disney hero.

00:02:49.501 --> 00:02:52.400
-And so I know we are both

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.300
huge fans of
"Brooklyn Nine-Nine."

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:55.968
-Yes.
-And it must've been so great

00:02:55.968 --> 00:02:58.167
to work with her on this.
I know you worked with her

00:02:58.167 --> 00:02:59.767
on "In the Heights" as well.
-Absolutely.

00:02:59.767 --> 00:03:02.834
Well, I think one of
the great cosmic jokes is

00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:05.567
that Stephanie became famous
on a show

00:03:05.567 --> 00:03:07.267
where her voice is
two octaves down

00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:08.734
and in a monotone.
-Yes.

00:03:08.734 --> 00:03:10.667
-And Detective Rosa Diaz.
When you actually meet her,

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:12.167
she's like, "Hi!"
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:14.000
And she has, like,
one of the bubbliest,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.934
most musical voices
you'll ever hear.

00:03:15.934 --> 00:03:18.367
So I knew that Stephanie.
I met Stephanie

00:03:18.367 --> 00:03:21.167
in our early 20s, mid 20s,
when she was doing

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.667
Quiara's play,
like, very experimental,

00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:25.601
off- off-, off-
off-Broadway play.

00:03:25.601 --> 00:03:28.434
So it's been a joy to --
It was amazing

00:03:28.434 --> 00:03:31.534
when she had her sort of
nationwide prominence

00:03:31.534 --> 00:03:34.000
on "Brooklyn Nine-Nine."
And then, you know,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:35.801
to work with her on this,

00:03:35.801 --> 00:03:37.501
like, where I'm writing
Disney songs

00:03:37.501 --> 00:03:40.167
and she's the Disney,
like, hero,

00:03:40.167 --> 00:03:42.868
I'm like, "She's in a club
with, like, Jodi Benson

00:03:42.868 --> 00:03:45.400
and Anika Noni Rose
and Lea Salonga

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:46.968
and all these, like,
Disney heroines."

00:03:46.968 --> 00:03:49.634
So, we would text each other

00:03:49.634 --> 00:03:51.067
while we were recording
her songs,

00:03:51.067 --> 00:03:52.667
like, "Can you believe this?
Can you believe this?"

00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:54.000
[ Laughter ]
It was great.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.968
-So, at this point,
it must be cool to have,

00:03:56.968 --> 00:03:58.467
like, a good
working relationship

00:03:58.467 --> 00:04:01.100
with something
as iconic as Disney,

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:02.767
which it introduces
so many children

00:04:02.767 --> 00:04:05.033
to musical theater, I feel.
-Yeah, 100%.

00:04:05.033 --> 00:04:07.767
I think a lot
about Howard Ashman,

00:04:07.767 --> 00:04:11.100
who was Alan Menken's
great lyricist and partner

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.100
and really, I think,
is responsible

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:16.200
for ushering in that, like,
second golden age of Disney.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.200
He wrote the lyrics
for "Little Mermaid"

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:19.334
and "Beauty and the Beast,"

00:04:19.334 --> 00:04:21.033
and really just, sort of

00:04:21.033 --> 00:04:23.300
brought musical theater
into animation.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.367
It was like, "This is
how it works, guys."

00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:29.167
And just was -- I was
really inspired by that.

00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:32.501
And so, you know, when
I started working on "Encanto,"

00:04:32.501 --> 00:04:34.000
I just said, "I want to be there
from the beginning,"

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.300
so that the songs and the scenes
can kind of

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.200
go as seamlessly into each other
as possible."

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.834
And you know, it's been --
I'm really proud

00:04:41.834 --> 00:04:43.234
of "Encanto" and
the work we did together.

00:04:43.234 --> 00:04:45.067
-And I'm sure, when you work

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:46.267
on a musical
on a Broadway stage,

00:04:46.267 --> 00:04:47.901
you see it at every level.

00:04:47.901 --> 00:04:49.834
There's no moment where
you're, you know, blown away

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:51.801
by what happened
between the night before

00:04:51.801 --> 00:04:54.133
and you know, the morning after.
-Right.

00:04:54.133 --> 00:04:55.767
-With animation,
it must be so thrilling

00:04:55.767 --> 00:04:58.067
the first time you see,
like, a song you wrote.

00:04:58.067 --> 00:04:59.334
-Yeah.
This is the movie

00:04:59.334 --> 00:05:01.701
I've seen the least
that I have worked on.

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:04.601
Also, because I think -- because
we were working from home,

00:05:04.601 --> 00:05:07.234
Disney security
was even more intense.

00:05:07.234 --> 00:05:11.434
So I would be like, "Can I just
get minutes 45 to 48?

00:05:11.434 --> 00:05:13.567
'Cause I have a write
a song for that?

00:05:13.567 --> 00:05:15.567
Can I see it, please?"

00:05:15.567 --> 00:05:20.267
And -- But it was also --
what's so thrilling

00:05:20.267 --> 00:05:21.567
about writing for animation is

00:05:21.567 --> 00:05:23.968
that they can move
at the speed of thought.

00:05:23.968 --> 00:05:27.267
So, there's a song in the movie
called "Surface Pressure,"

00:05:27.267 --> 00:05:30.634
where, you know,
this character is singing

00:05:30.634 --> 00:05:33.534
about how she feels nervous
and what's under the surface.

00:05:33.534 --> 00:05:36.267
So, again, I cut to my kids
worried about me,

00:05:36.267 --> 00:05:37.467
and me in the corner,
being like...

00:05:37.467 --> 00:05:42.367
"Nervous," "surface,"
"how big the iceberg is."

00:05:42.367 --> 00:05:44.901
[ Laughter ]
"Cerberus?"

00:05:44.901 --> 00:05:48.434
And just as many "nervous"
rhymes as I can get,

00:05:48.434 --> 00:05:50.868
and I write the song.
And then, when it comes back,

00:05:50.868 --> 00:05:54.167
the animation goes there.
It can show you Cerberus.

00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:55.934
It can show
how big the iceberg is.

00:05:55.934 --> 00:05:57.400
And it can move
at the speed of thought,

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.267
which is just thrilling
when you're a lyricist.

00:05:59.267 --> 00:06:00.968
-That's really exciting.
I want to --

00:06:00.968 --> 00:06:03.000
I'm very excited to talk to you
about your directorial debut.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.300
We're going to be right back

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:05.634
with more
from Lin-Manuel Miranda.

